{"name":"Violet Scent Soft Blue","hex":"#bcc6df","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"violet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant or flower of the genus Viola, especially the fragrant Viola odorata;  similar-looking plants and flowers.","A person thought to resemble V. odorata, especially in its beauty and delicacy."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a violet colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/violet"},{"term":"scent","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A distinctive smell.","A smell left by an animal that may be used for tracing."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To detect the scent of; to discern by the sense of smell.","To inhale in order to detect the scent of (something)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/scent"},{"term":"soft","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Easily giving way under pressure.","Smooth and flexible; not rough, rugged, or harsh."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Be quiet; hold; stop; not so fast."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soft"}],"entries":[{"title":"Odor","blocks":[{"text":"An odor or odour is a smell or a scent caused by one or more volatilized chemical compounds generally found in low concentrations that humans and many animals can perceive via their olfactory system. While smell can refer to pleasant and unpleasant odors, the terms scent, aroma, and fragrance are usually reserved for pleasant-smelling odors and are frequently used in the food and cosmetic industry to describe floral scents or to refer to perfumes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Jan_Brueghel_d._O._-_Allegorie_van_de_geur.jpg/330px-Jan_Brueghel_d._O._-_Allegorie_van_de_geur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odor"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"},{"title":"Violet (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Violet is the color of light at the short wavelength end of the visible spectrum. It is one of the seven colors that Isaac Newton labeled when dividing the spectrum of visible light in 1704. Violet light has a wavelength between approximately 380 and 435 nanometers. The color's name is derived from the Viola genus of flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Violet_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/ALCOSAN_Odor_Control_Covers_%286926400399%29.jpg/500px-ALCOSAN_Odor_Control_Covers_%286926400399%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/ALCOSAN_Odor_Control_Covers_%286926400399%29.jpg/500px-ALCOSAN_Odor_Control_Covers_%286926400399%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Odor control covers at a sewage treatment plant: Under these covers, grit and gravel are settled out 